              ":question/problem": "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(v)-v \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(v)-v}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
