          ":tutorial/content": "Given any homogeneous first-order ordinary differential equation, we can always transform it into a separable equation through the substitution $y=vx,$ where $v=v(x)$ is a function of $x.$\n\nTo understand why, suppose we have a homogeneous first-order ordinary differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y).\n$$\n\nThen because $f$ is homogeneous of degree zero, we have\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nfor any $t.$ By letting $t=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =f(tx,ty) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x,\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =F\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the function $F$ is defined as $F(v) = f(1,v).$ \n\nNow, letting $v = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ (or equivalently, $y=vx$), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =F\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(vx) & =F(v) \\\\ v+x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =F(v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is now separable! We can separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =F(v)-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{F(v)-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
