          ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
            ":db/id": 17592186087458,
            ":question/id": "f34de8cd-b650-5926-9f06-8127d4fb3a55",
            ":question/math-academy-id": "e-2393",
            ":question/problem": "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$",
            ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""
          },
